sec.p:
Based on this general process model, the 4 provinces of Ilam, Alborz, Sistan, and North Khorasan, as well as South Khorasan, which have the highest counts of out-of-province patient travel, are outstanding. Based on the numbers obtained from the process model, the inter-provincial mobility rates for visiting doctors and purchasing medicine are tabulated in Table 2, columns 2 and 3, respectively. The mobility rate is the difference between the count of people whose place of insurance and place of visit vary, divided by the total patient population in a given province. According to Table 2 content, the provinces with the lowest mobility indicate a proper distribution of medical resources and specialists, which are Fars, Isfahan, Khorasan Razavi, Gilan, Yazd, and Qom, respectively, with mobility rates of <0.1. Tehran province, with a population of nearly 15 million, has a high travel rate. The average age of the statistical population here is over 60. It is natural that the insured in Tehran, capable of living outside the megacity, will go to the neighboring provinces. This phenomenon is observed in the third column of Table 2, where the similarity in the count of visiting province and the province of receiving drugs (
≅600
) reveals that the patient has chosen to reside in a province other than Tehran. The provinces with mobility rates >0.4 are Ilam, Sistan and Baluchestan, South Khorasan, North Khorasan, Alborz, Lorestan, and Bushehr, respectively. The Hormozgan, Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari, Kohgiluyeh, Khuzestan, and Qazvin provinces also have mobility rates between 0.3 and 0.4. The count of travels to receive medicine is low in all provinces except South and North Khorasan and Sistan. The fourth column in Table 2 represents the referral rates from other provinces to a special province, which has been obtained by the difference of the total number of travels to a province (the number on the incoming edge) and the total number of patients in a province (the number on the node), divided by the total statistical population. The process model for each province is extracted separately, and then the values of its edges and nodes are obtained accurately. The top 3 provinces with the highest number of referrals from other provinces that provide medical services to cross-provincial patients are Tehran, Alborz, and Isfahan, accounting for 50%, 11%, and 12%, respectively, which constitutes 73% of the total statistical population in this context. East Azarbaijan, Khorasan Razavi, Fars, and Qom have a relatively high count of referrals, while Khorasan Shomali, Ilam, Sistan, Kordestan, and Kohgiloyeh have a low count of referrals. The negative values in Table 2 indicate provinces as origin points for outbound medical travel, with no recorded inbound medical visits from other provinces. As to North Khorasan, the negative values in both physician visits and medicine purchases indicate that no inbound medical visits are recorded in this province, while these negative values serve as a primary o North Khorasan, the negative values in both physician visits and medicine purchases confirm that no inbound medical visits are recorded in this province, while these negative values serve exclusively as a source of outbound medical travel.
